title op3: an empty directory has no voters and concludes nothing

# Vacuous fullness, put beside a directory that does conclude, so the
# difference is a few lines of gold apart.
#
# Off-of renames both directories the way a rebuild does it, giving
# each a fresh dnode.  /empty has no children; /full has one, and it
# keeps its dnode.  Onto is quiet.
#
# /full's single voter votes into /filled by red, /filled is the
# unique target, and the move is believed: the output keeps onto's
# dnode 3 under the new name.
#
# /empty has no voting children at all.  Fullness holds toward every
# candidate vacuously, and the distinction that matters is that this
# does not FAIL -- it asserts nothing.  A rule that concluded from an
# empty vote would conclude /empty moved to /filled just as readily
# as to /emptied, since both are candidates by the sibling generator
# and neither has a single voter to separate them.  So /empty dies
# and /emptied is born with a fresh dnode.
#
# That asymmetry is successor question S4: whether the decay of empty
# and emptied directories is acceptable in practice or wants evidence
# of its own.  This fixture is what makes the cost concrete, and the
# cost is small -- one directory's identity per empty directory
# renamed, with nothing underneath to lose.
#
# Note the trailing slash on the empty directory in the gold block.
# It is not decoration: with no children and no content token, the
# slash is the only thing that says the pool is a directory.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: empty/
    3-100: full/
      4-100: f = fv0

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: empty/
    3-100: full/
      4-100: f = fv0

tree offof
  1-1: /
    10-300: emptied/
    11-300: filled/
      4-100: f = fv0

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    10-300: emptied/
    3-100: filled
      4-100: f = fv0
